1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,943
Session five.

2
00:00:01,223 --> 00:00:03,064
The evolution of HTTP.

3
00:00:03,344 --> 00:00:06,862
Same words, three completely different
ways of writing them down.

4
00:00:07,142 --> 00:00:08,667
The protocol did not drift.

5
00:00:08,947 --> 00:00:09,740
It was pushed.

6
00:00:10,666 --> 00:00:12,437
The course runs in five parts.

7
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:14,571
Each version ends on its own wall.

8
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:21,195
HTTP one point zero, RFC nineteen forty-
five, May nineteen ninety-six.

9
00:00:21,475 --> 00:00:23,660
It ends on one response per connection.

10
00:00:23,999 --> 00:00:30,083
Early HTTP one point one, RFC twenty sixty-
eight, January nineteen ninety-seven.

11
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:32,944
It ends on the rule: at most two connections.

12
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:35,408
HTTP one point one today.

13
00:00:35,688 --> 00:00:38,863
RFC twenty six sixteen in nineteen ninety-nine,

14
00:00:38,863 --> 00:00:43,593
then seventy-two thirty to seventy-two
thirty-five in twenty fourteen,

15
00:00:43,593 --> 00:00:47,783
then ninety-one ten to ninety-one
twelve in twenty twenty-two.

16
00:00:48,063 --> 00:00:49,803
It ends on head-of-line blocking.

17
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:51,496
HTTP two.

18
00:00:51,776 --> 00:00:54,952
RFC seventy-five forty, May twenty fifteen,

19
00:00:54,952 --> 00:00:58,866
replaced by ninety-one thirteen
in twenty twenty-two,

20
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:01,895
with HPACK in RFC seventy-five forty-one.

21
00:01:02,175 --> 00:01:04,618
It ends on TCP head-of-line blocking.

22
00:01:04,966 --> 00:01:06,754
And QUIC with HTTP three.

23
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:12,058
RFC ninety-thousand for QUIC, ninety-
one fourteen for HTTP three,

24
00:01:12,058 --> 00:01:16,929
ninety-oh-oh-one for QUIC TLS, and
ninety-two-oh-four for QPACK.

25
00:01:17,209 --> 00:01:21,248
It ends on the three to five percent
of networks that block UDP.

26
00:01:22,166 --> 00:01:26,168
HTTP one point zero, the whole
protocol, on one slide.

27
00:01:26,499 --> 00:01:28,428
Three methods: GET, HEAD and POST.

28
00:01:28,708 --> 00:01:33,092
PUT, DELETE, LINK and UNLINK appear in an
appendix of additional methods.

29
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:36,937
Sixteen status codes, from two
hundred through five oh three.

30
00:01:37,217 --> 00:01:39,011
Today, sixty-two are assigned.

31
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:40,615
And just sixteen headers.

32
00:01:40,895 --> 00:01:47,822
Allow, Authorization, Content-Encoding, Content-
Length, Content-Type, Date, Expires, From,

33
00:01:47,822 --> 00:01:53,912
If-Modified-Since, Last-Modified, Location,
Pragma, Referer, Server, User-Agent,

34
00:01:53,912 --> 00:01:55,511
and WWW-Authenticate.

35
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:57,675
You can speak it by hand with netcat.

36
00:01:57,955 --> 00:02:03,214
The response, a blank line as delimiter, the body,
and then the server closes with a FIN.

37
00:02:03,494 --> 00:02:08,109
curl, by the way, adds four headers you did not
write, and hides two the server sent.

38
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:10,143
Four limitations.

39
00:02:10,423 --> 00:02:13,004
One: the socket dies after one response.

40
00:02:14,033 --> 00:02:17,056
RFC nineteen forty-five says the connection is

41
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:22,182
established by the client before each
request, and closed by the server after.

42
00:02:22,499 --> 00:02:23,457
One request.

43
00:02:23,799 --> 00:02:26,128
One response, and then the server hangs up.

44
00:02:26,466 --> 00:02:31,009
A second request on the same socket gets
back an empty read, bytes b-empty.

45
00:02:31,289 --> 00:02:32,152
Not an error.

46
00:02:32,432 --> 00:02:33,286
Just nothing.

47
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:38,295
The arithmetic: a twenty-four object page at
two hundred milliseconds round trip.

48
00:02:38,575 --> 00:02:41,497
Twenty-four handshakes cost four
point eight seconds.

49
00:02:41,777 --> 00:02:45,738
Twenty-four request and response pairs
cost another four point eight.

50
00:02:46,018 --> 00:02:49,723
Nine point six seconds, plus twenty-
four congestion windows,

51
00:02:49,723 --> 00:02:52,213
each starting from nothing in slow start.

52
00:02:53,133 --> 00:02:55,011
Limitations two, three and four.

53
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:55,869
Two.

54
00:02:56,149 --> 00:02:59,560
No Host header, so one IP
address means one website.

55
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,614
Until January nineteen ninety-seven
the ratio was one to one.

56
00:03:03,894 --> 00:03:06,751
Today there are about one point
one billion websites.

57
00:03:07,031 --> 00:03:10,899
The fix, the Host header, is the highest
leverage line in the standard.

58
00:03:11,233 --> 00:03:11,819
Three.

59
00:03:12,099 --> 00:03:14,250
Caching, with a clock as the fingerprint.

60
00:03:14,530 --> 00:03:19,071
Last-Modified and If-Modified-Since give
you a three oh four Not Modified,

61
00:03:19,071 --> 00:03:21,955
and two hundred two thousand bytes become zero.

62
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:28,143
But timestamps have no sub-second field, clocks
drift between you, me and the proxy,

63
00:03:28,143 --> 00:03:34,050
and a modification time is not identity: a
rebuild changes every mtime but no bytes.

64
00:03:34,399 --> 00:03:34,953
Four.

65
00:03:35,233 --> 00:03:37,338
Only sixteen headers and three methods.

66
00:03:37,618 --> 00:03:41,261
There is no OPTIONS, so you cannot ask
a server what it supports.

67
00:03:42,199 --> 00:03:45,472
Every one of these is fixed in
RFC twenty sixty-eight.

68
00:03:46,499 --> 00:03:48,817
Persistence, in section eight point one.

69
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:53,432
More headers and methods, including
OPTIONS, in section nine point two.

70
00:03:53,766 --> 00:03:56,318
ETag, in section fourteen point nineteen.

71
00:03:56,666 --> 00:03:59,424
And Host, in section fourteen point twenty-three.

72
00:03:59,766 --> 00:04:00,726
One correction.

73
00:04:01,006 --> 00:04:05,472
HTTP one point zero does not really
have head-of-line blocking.

74
00:04:05,752 --> 00:04:09,714
HOL needs a queue, and one point
zero has no pipelining.

75
00:04:09,994 --> 00:04:13,545
It has a serialisation cost: one
round trip per object.

76
00:04:13,825 --> 00:04:18,744
Real head-of-line blocking arrives in one
point one, as a side effect of the fix.

77
00:04:19,024 --> 00:04:21,754
Every solution here is the
cause of the next slide.

78
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:24,631
Early HTTP one point one.

79
00:04:24,911 --> 00:04:27,125
First, persistence becomes the default.

80
00:04:27,466 --> 00:04:31,926
In one point zero, Connection keep-alive
was a non-standard extension.

81
00:04:32,206 --> 00:04:35,286
Some servers honoured it, some
ignored it, and some crashed.

82
00:04:35,566 --> 00:04:37,063
The default was to hang up.

83
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,575
In one point one, persistent
connections are the default.

84
00:04:40,855 --> 00:04:42,567
You opt out with Connection close.

85
00:04:42,847 --> 00:04:44,212
The default is to stay.

86
00:04:44,492 --> 00:04:47,166
Three responses, one TCP handshake.

87
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:51,211
The Host header, from section
fourteen point twenty-three.

88
00:04:51,491 --> 00:04:53,741
One IP address, a thousand sites.

89
00:04:54,021 --> 00:04:55,440
This is virtual hosting.

90
00:04:55,766 --> 00:05:00,733
In one point one, a request with no Host
header gets a four hundred Bad Request.

91
00:05:01,013 --> 00:05:01,892
It is a MUST.

92
00:05:02,172 --> 00:05:05,101
Same IP, same port, same socket, different sites.

93
00:05:06,133 --> 00:05:07,937
It is the same move at four layers.

94
00:05:08,217 --> 00:05:13,220
In HTTP one point zero, the four-tuple was
the address, so the fix was Host:

95
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,064
put the name in the message.

96
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:20,719
In TLS, the Host header is encrypted, but the
certificate must be chosen first.

97
00:05:20,999 --> 00:05:24,537
So the name goes in the
ClientHello, in the clear, as SNI.

98
00:05:24,866 --> 00:05:27,995
In HTTP two, headers are compressed and unordered,

99
00:05:27,995 --> 00:05:30,812
so there is a pseudo-header called authority.

100
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:36,162
And elsewhere, many destinations over one
connection: a topic, a routing key,

101
00:05:36,162 --> 00:05:37,534
an ingress host rule.

102
00:05:38,466 --> 00:05:39,180
ETag.

103
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:41,034
A fingerprint, not a clock.

104
00:05:42,066 --> 00:05:43,780
The server returns an ETag,

105
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:48,922
for example the first sixteen hex characters
of a sha two fifty-six of the bytes.

106
00:05:49,266 --> 00:05:51,390
The client sends it back in If-None-Match.

107
00:05:51,733 --> 00:05:53,903
And if it still matches, a three oh four.

108
00:05:54,183 --> 00:05:57,171
Two hundred two thousand bytes
of body become zero.

109
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:03,067
A weak validator, written W/and a quoted string,
means equivalent, not byte-identical.

110
00:06:03,347 --> 00:06:05,127
Range requests need a strong one.

111
00:06:05,466 --> 00:06:07,619
If-Match is the same idea for writes.

112
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:11,630
It gives you optimistic concurrency,
the fix for lost updates.

113
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:14,145
Accept-Encoding, and Vary.

114
00:06:14,466 --> 00:06:18,844
Asking for identity encoding, the body is
two hundred two thousand bytes.

115
00:06:19,166 --> 00:06:22,409
With gzip, five thousand seven
hundred sixty-three bytes.

116
00:06:22,689 --> 00:06:24,277
That is two point eight percent.

117
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:26,126
Deflate is about the same.

118
00:06:26,466 --> 00:06:31,852
So the claim that HTTP one point one is slow
because it is text is undercut:

119
00:06:31,852 --> 00:06:32,986
text compresses.

120
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:35,063
The body was never the problem.

121
00:06:35,343 --> 00:06:39,180
The headers were, because Content-
Encoding compresses only the body.

122
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:43,856
And Vary is a cache key instruction: this
URL has more than one answer,

123
00:06:43,856 --> 00:06:45,758
and here is what it depends on.

124
00:06:46,038 --> 00:06:48,029
Vary Accept-Encoding is correct.

125
00:06:48,309 --> 00:06:52,997
Vary User-Agent creates millions of keys
and sends the hit rate to zero.

126
00:06:53,277 --> 00:06:55,033
Vary star turns caching off.

127
00:06:55,966 --> 00:06:56,600
Range.

128
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,685
The header that runs your data lake.

129
00:06:59,733 --> 00:07:04,237
Bytes one hundred to one ninety-nine
returns two oh six Partial Content,

130
00:07:04,237 --> 00:07:08,241
with a Content-Range header giving the
slice and the total size.

131
00:07:08,566 --> 00:07:13,498
Bytes minus fifty means the last fifty bytes, and
you do not even need to know the size.

132
00:07:13,833 --> 00:07:18,094
Asking beyond the end returns four
sixteen, Range Not Satisfiable.

133
00:07:18,374 --> 00:07:22,893
That code was added in RFC twenty six
sixteen, not twenty sixty-eight.

134
00:07:23,233 --> 00:07:28,509
Who uses it: video scrubbing, resumable
downloads, and Parquet and ZIP footers.

135
00:07:28,789 --> 00:07:33,098
The directory is at the end of the file, so
you read the last few kilobytes,

136
00:07:33,098 --> 00:07:35,196
then fetch only the columns you need.

137
00:07:36,133 --> 00:07:39,722
Chunked transfer encoding: a body
without knowing its length.

138
00:07:40,066 --> 00:07:43,853
The header says Transfer-Encoding chunked,
and there is no Content-Length.

139
00:07:44,133 --> 00:07:48,733
Each chunk starts with its length in hex:
thirty-seven hex is fifty-five bytes.

140
00:07:49,013 --> 00:07:49,868
Then the data.

141
00:07:50,148 --> 00:07:52,626
And an empty chunk, zero, ends the body.

142
00:07:52,966 --> 00:07:55,216
That is both length and delimiter, nested.

143
00:07:55,496 --> 00:08:00,017
Each chunk is length-prefixed, and the
sequence is delimited by an empty chunk.

144
00:08:00,297 --> 00:08:03,518
It is the same structure as FastCGI records.

145
00:08:03,866 --> 00:08:08,639
It underpins streaming JSON, server-
sent events, and gRPC trailers.

146
00:08:09,566 --> 00:08:12,084
Everything else that arrived in one point one.

147
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:15,277
OPTIONS asks what a server supports.

148
00:08:15,557 --> 00:08:17,589
You meet it in every CORS preflight.

149
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:19,884
PUT and DELETE: write and remove.

150
00:08:20,164 --> 00:08:21,283
REST APIs.

151
00:08:21,633 --> 00:08:24,550
TRACE echoes the request back, including proxies.

152
00:08:24,830 --> 00:08:29,126
It is disabled everywhere, because of Cross-
Site Tracing in two thousand three.

153
00:08:29,466 --> 00:08:31,528
Via: each proxy appends itself.

154
00:08:31,808 --> 00:08:34,238
Useful for debugging which box is lying.

155
00:08:34,566 --> 00:08:39,011
Expect 100-continue lets you ask
before uploading four gigabytes.

156
00:08:39,291 --> 00:08:41,904
It is why curl pauses for a second on big PUTs.

157
00:08:42,233 --> 00:08:48,915
Cache-Control replaced one timestamp with a
vocabulary: max-age, s-maxage for shared caches,

158
00:08:48,915 --> 00:08:50,295
no-cache, no-store.

159
00:08:50,633 --> 00:08:52,294
And about forty status codes.

160
00:08:52,574 --> 00:08:55,800
Five oh four was already in RFC
twenty sixty-eight,

161
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:59,976
so the whole five hundred family
existed in nineteen ninety-seven.

162
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:02,490
No-cache means revalidate before use.

163
00:09:02,770 --> 00:09:05,088
No-store means do not write it down at all.

164
00:09:05,368 --> 00:09:07,653
Everybody gets this wrong exactly once.

165
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:10,790
Section eight point one point four said a

166
00:09:10,790 --> 00:09:13,621
client should not maintain more
than two connections.

167
00:09:13,901 --> 00:09:17,492
Here is six assets, at one hundred
fifty milliseconds round trip.

168
00:09:17,833 --> 00:09:21,280
Closing after each: twelve round
trips, six connections,

169
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:23,803
thirteen hundred sixty-nine milliseconds.

170
00:09:24,133 --> 00:09:28,564
Keep-alive on one connection: seven round
trips, nine hundred eighty-five.

171
00:09:28,900 --> 00:09:34,150
Two parallel connections, as the RFC says:
four round trips, six hundred seven.

172
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:39,184
Six parallel connections, as browsers did:
two round trips, six hundred eight.

173
00:09:39,464 --> 00:09:42,259
The same wall time as two, with more connections.

174
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:46,104
And pipelined in one batch: two
round trips, one connection,

175
00:09:46,104 --> 00:09:47,973
three hundred four milliseconds.

176
00:09:48,253 --> 00:09:49,099
The best row.

177
00:09:49,433 --> 00:09:53,986
Browsers shipped six connections per
origin by around two thousand eight,

178
00:09:53,986 --> 00:09:57,915
and eighteen with domain sharding:
img one, img two, img three.

179
00:09:58,195 --> 00:10:02,392
RFC seventy-two thirty removed the
sentence in twenty fourteen.

180
00:10:02,672 --> 00:10:03,841
The spec conceded.

181
00:10:04,166 --> 00:10:06,946
Pipelining was the best row,
and nobody shipped it.

182
00:10:07,866 --> 00:10:12,774
RFC twenty six sixteen, June nineteen
ninety-nine, was mostly tightening.

183
00:10:13,054 --> 00:10:18,973
It added three oh seven, four sixteen, four
seventeen with Expect, identity content-coding,

184
00:10:18,973 --> 00:10:20,339
and made Host a MUST.

185
00:10:20,619 --> 00:10:22,119
Five oh four is not new.

186
00:10:22,466 --> 00:10:24,413
Why does 307 exist?

187
00:10:24,693 --> 00:10:27,513
It is a standard losing to deployed reality.

188
00:10:27,793 --> 00:10:31,624
RFC twenty sixty-eight said a three
oh two keeps the method,

189
00:10:31,624 --> 00:10:34,561
but every browser turned POST into GET anyway.

190
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:39,372
So twenty six sixteen renamed three oh two
to Found, documented the reality,

191
00:10:39,372 --> 00:10:42,020
and added three oh seven for keep the method.

192
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:45,375
Three oh three, See Other,
explicitly means change to GET.

193
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:49,680
It is easier to add a number than to fix
a billion deployed clients.

194
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:51,905
Postel's law loses.

195
00:10:52,233 --> 00:10:55,380
RFC twenty six sixteen, in nineteen ninety-nine:

196
00:10:55,380 --> 00:11:01,214
if both Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length are
present, Content-Length must be ignored.

197
00:11:01,494 --> 00:11:02,892
Recover, don't reject.

198
00:11:03,172 --> 00:11:04,750
Be liberal in what you accept.

199
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:10,712
RFC ninety-one twelve, in twenty twenty-two:
the server may reject such a request,

200
00:11:10,712 --> 00:11:13,518
or process it by Transfer-Encoding alone,

201
00:11:13,518 --> 00:11:17,693
but regardless it must close the
connection after responding.

202
00:11:18,033 --> 00:11:21,816
Be liberal in what you accept is a
fine rule for two programs.

203
00:11:22,096 --> 00:11:24,234
It is catastrophic for a chain of five.

204
00:11:25,166 --> 00:11:26,973
Which brings us to request smuggling.

205
00:11:27,253 --> 00:11:29,049
One byte stream, two readings.

206
00:11:29,366 --> 00:11:34,584
Here is a request with both headers: Content-
Length six, and Transfer-Encoding chunked.

207
00:11:34,864 --> 00:11:37,215
Then a chunk of zero, which ends the body.

208
00:11:37,495 --> 00:11:40,730
And then bytes that look like a
second request: GET/admin.

209
00:11:41,066 --> 00:11:46,684
A reader that trusts Content-Length sees one
request, with a body of zero, carriage return,

210
00:11:46,684 --> 00:11:50,141
line feed, carriage return, line
feed, and the letter G.

211
00:11:50,466 --> 00:11:53,670
A reader that trusts Transfer-
Encoding sees two requests.

212
00:11:53,950 --> 00:11:55,464
And the second one is GET/admin.

213
00:11:55,800 --> 00:12:01,447
Now put a CDN in front, which reads one way, and an
origin behind it, which reads the other.

214
00:12:01,727 --> 00:12:04,509
The origin sees a request the CDN never saw,

215
00:12:04,509 --> 00:12:08,618
and a spare response is left in the
connection for the next user.

216
00:12:08,898 --> 00:12:12,236
The demo server answered two
responses: a two hundred,

217
00:12:12,236 --> 00:12:15,141
and a four oh four for the request nobody sent.

218
00:12:15,421 --> 00:12:20,401
HTTP two makes this unrepresentable: the
length is in the frame header,

219
00:12:20,401 --> 00:12:22,014
with no second opinion.

220
00:12:22,933 --> 00:12:26,218
In June twenty fourteen, one document became six.

221
00:12:26,498 --> 00:12:28,457
Microservices for RFCs.

222
00:12:29,499 --> 00:12:35,221
Seventy-two thirty, message syntax and routing,
for proxies, load balancers and CDNs.

223
00:12:35,566 --> 00:12:39,307
Seventy-two thirty-one, semantics and
content, for frameworks.

224
00:12:39,633 --> 00:12:44,030
Seventy-two thirty-two, conditional
requests, for anything with an ETag.

225
00:12:44,366 --> 00:12:48,809
Seventy-two thirty-three, range requests,
for video, downloads and Parquet.

226
00:12:49,133 --> 00:12:52,393
Seventy-two thirty-four, caching, for every CDN.

227
00:12:52,733 --> 00:12:55,270
And seventy-two thirty-five, authentication.

228
00:12:56,199 --> 00:12:58,753
Why is your JWT base sixty-four?

229
00:12:59,099 --> 00:13:03,746
The HTTP token grammar excludes a list
of punctuation characters.

230
00:13:04,026 --> 00:13:09,997
That rule comes from RFC twenty six sixteen, section
two point two, in nineteen ninety-nine.

231
00:13:10,333 --> 00:13:15,424
A JWT must survive the Authorization
header, a URL query, a cookie,

232
00:13:15,424 --> 00:13:20,060
a form field and a JSON body, and
its signature is raw bytes.

233
00:13:20,399 --> 00:13:23,556
So base sixty-four URL swaps plus
and/for -and underscore.

234
00:13:23,836 --> 00:13:28,792
Every layer has an alphabet, and the intersection
is about sixty-four characters wide.

235
00:13:29,072 --> 00:13:33,178
That is why base sixty-four exists,
same as MIME in session two.

236
00:13:34,099 --> 00:13:38,684
In June twenty twenty-two, the standard
was reorganised around one idea:

237
00:13:38,684 --> 00:13:41,040
semantics are separate from encoding.

238
00:13:42,066 --> 00:13:48,909
Ninety-one ten is HTTP semantics: methods, status
codes, headers, ranges, authentication.

239
00:13:49,189 --> 00:13:50,555
Not version specific.

240
00:13:50,835 --> 00:13:53,067
Shared by one point one, two and three.

241
00:13:53,399 --> 00:13:56,014
Ninety-one eleven is caching, also shared.

242
00:13:56,333 --> 00:13:59,407
Ninety-one twelve is HTTP one point one.

243
00:13:59,687 --> 00:14:00,901
Wire format only.

244
00:14:01,233 --> 00:14:03,936
Ninety-one thirteen is HTTP two.

245
00:14:04,216 --> 00:14:05,430
Wire format only.

246
00:14:05,766 --> 00:14:08,635
And ninety-one fourteen is HTTP three.

247
00:14:08,915 --> 00:14:10,129
Wire format only.

248
00:14:10,466 --> 00:14:12,226
Three encodings, one language.

249
00:14:12,506 --> 00:14:17,443
And, a fun fact: four eighteen, I'm a
teapot, comes from an April Fool's RFC,

250
00:14:17,443 --> 00:14:20,712
twenty three twenty-four, in
nineteen ninety-eight.

251
00:14:20,992 --> 00:14:24,364
It is reserved forever, because too
many people shipped the joke.

252
00:14:25,299 --> 00:14:27,951
Now, what HTTP one point one cost.

253
00:14:28,231 --> 00:14:29,807
First, head-of-line blocking.

254
00:14:30,133 --> 00:14:32,503
Pipeline six requests on one connection,

255
00:14:32,503 --> 00:14:35,999
where the first asks for a response
that takes two seconds.

256
00:14:36,279 --> 00:14:39,940
Every response arrives at about two
thousand three milliseconds.

257
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:43,216
Five fast responses wait two seconds for a sixth.

258
00:14:44,966 --> 00:14:49,166
With one connection each, the big one still
takes two thousand six milliseconds,

259
00:14:49,499 --> 00:14:51,206
but the others take three to seven.

260
00:14:51,486 --> 00:14:52,512
Hardly anything.

261
00:14:52,833 --> 00:14:56,140
The cause: there is no request
identifier on the wire.

262
00:14:56,420 --> 00:14:58,190
The order is the identifier.

263
00:14:58,470 --> 00:15:02,550
Responses must come back in request order,
so the slowest sets the pace.

264
00:15:03,499 --> 00:15:05,350
Multiplexing needs identity.

265
00:15:05,630 --> 00:15:07,626
Compare how every protocol does it.

266
00:15:08,666 --> 00:15:11,728
TCP uses source and destination ports.

267
00:15:12,066 --> 00:15:15,224
SS7 TCAP uses a transaction id.

268
00:15:15,566 --> 00:15:18,989
FastCGI puts a request id in every record.

269
00:15:19,333 --> 00:15:23,333
HTTP one point one has nothing, so
it needs strict ordering.

270
00:15:23,666 --> 00:15:29,115
HTTP two and three put a thirty-one bit
stream identifier in every frame.

271
00:15:30,033 --> 00:15:31,595
Second cost: header bloat.

272
00:15:31,933 --> 00:15:36,820
One request's headers, with cookies, User-
Agent, Accept and sec-fetch headers,

273
00:15:36,820 --> 00:15:38,861
come to eight hundred five bytes.

274
00:15:39,141 --> 00:15:42,733
Eighty requests make sixty-four thousand
three hundred twenty bytes.

275
00:15:43,013 --> 00:15:47,676
The new information, the paths, is sixteen
hundred bytes: two point five percent.

276
00:15:47,956 --> 00:15:51,786
Ninety-seven point five percent of
what you upload is a repeat.

277
00:15:52,066 --> 00:15:56,754
On a one megabit uplink, that is half a second
before any application data moves.

278
00:15:57,099 --> 00:15:58,848
Why not just gzip the headers?

279
00:15:59,128 --> 00:16:00,086
Because of CRIME.

280
00:16:00,366 --> 00:16:01,332
We will get to it.

281
00:16:02,266 --> 00:16:04,886
Third cost: when keep-alive made things slower.

282
00:16:05,166 --> 00:16:06,687
Nagle plus delayed ACK.

283
00:16:07,033 --> 00:16:11,292
Nagle's algorithm, RFC eight ninety-
six, from nineteen eighty-four:

284
00:16:11,292 --> 00:16:15,489
do not send a small segment while an
earlier one is unacknowledged.

285
00:16:15,769 --> 00:16:21,749
And delayed ACK, RFC eleven twenty-two: wait up to
forty milliseconds to piggyback the ACK.

286
00:16:22,066 --> 00:16:24,981
Now a server that does write
head, then write body.

287
00:16:25,261 --> 00:16:26,297
The head goes out.

288
00:16:26,577 --> 00:16:28,154
The body is held by Nagle.

289
00:16:28,434 --> 00:16:29,922
The client delays its ACK.

290
00:16:30,202 --> 00:16:34,868
So each request pays about forty
milliseconds, every request but the first,

291
00:16:34,868 --> 00:16:38,351
and only on persistent
connections, since close flushes.

292
00:16:38,699 --> 00:16:41,010
Total: one hundred seventy-four milliseconds,

293
00:16:41,333 --> 00:16:45,684
versus three point two milliseconds
with one write and TCP_NODELAY.

294
00:16:45,964 --> 00:16:50,149
nginx and Apache coalesce writes
and set TCP_NODELAY.

295
00:16:51,066 --> 00:16:53,291
The bill for HTTP one point one.

296
00:16:54,333 --> 00:16:58,059
Head-of-line blocking: two thousand
three milliseconds against three.

297
00:16:58,339 --> 00:16:59,718
Fixed by stream ids.

298
00:17:00,066 --> 00:17:03,123
Header bloat: ninety-seven point
five percent repeats.

299
00:17:03,403 --> 00:17:04,572
Fixed by HPACK.

300
00:17:04,899 --> 00:17:08,638
Too many connections: six per
origin, eighteen with sharding.

301
00:17:08,918 --> 00:17:10,390
Fixed by one connection.

302
00:17:10,733 --> 00:17:13,077
Ambiguous framing: request smuggling.

303
00:17:13,357 --> 00:17:14,744
Fixed by frame headers.

304
00:17:15,066 --> 00:17:18,698
And TCP itself: three round trips
before the first byte.

305
00:17:18,978 --> 00:17:19,984
Fixed by QUIC.

306
00:17:20,333 --> 00:17:22,314
Text is slow is not the finding.

307
00:17:22,594 --> 00:17:23,845
Gzip proved that.

308
00:17:24,125 --> 00:17:27,568
The problem was text without lengths
and without identifiers.

309
00:17:27,848 --> 00:17:31,519
HTTP two is HTTP with lengths and ids.

310
00:17:31,799 --> 00:17:33,442
Binary is the consequence.

311
00:17:33,722 --> 00:17:36,564
And text framing is exactly why HTTP won:

312
00:17:36,564 --> 00:17:40,098
anyone could implement it and
debug it with telnet.

313
00:17:41,033 --> 00:17:42,421
Now HTTP two.

314
00:17:42,701 --> 00:17:45,926
The history: ship it to a billion
users, then standardise.

315
00:17:46,266 --> 00:17:50,774
November two thousand nine: SPDY is
announced, by Belshe and Peon,

316
00:17:50,774 --> 00:17:53,574
claiming up to fifty-five percent faster.

317
00:17:53,899 --> 00:17:58,007
Between twenty ten and twenty
fourteen, Chrome, Firefox, nginx,

318
00:17:58,007 --> 00:17:59,898
Twitter and Facebook ship it.

319
00:18:00,233 --> 00:18:04,540
February twenty fifteen: Google
announces SPDY's retirement.

320
00:18:04,866 --> 00:18:10,723
May twenty fifteen: RFC seventy-five forty,
and seventy-five forty-one for HPACK.

321
00:18:11,003 --> 00:18:13,203
SPDY with the edges filed off.

322
00:18:13,533 --> 00:18:17,768
May twenty sixteen: SPDY is
removed in Chrome fifty-one.

323
00:18:18,099 --> 00:18:23,684
June twenty twenty-two: RFC ninety-one
thirteen obsoletes seventy-five forty,

324
00:18:23,684 --> 00:18:27,020
and deprecates priorities and the h2c Upgrade.

325
00:18:27,366 --> 00:18:30,339
And it is HTTP/two, not two point zero.

326
00:18:30,619 --> 00:18:31,854
No minor versions.

327
00:18:32,134 --> 00:18:35,214
The next version is a new number
and a new wire format.

328
00:18:36,133 --> 00:18:40,583
Every HTTP two connection starts with
a twenty-four byte preface.

329
00:18:40,899 --> 00:18:46,334
It is the text: PRI, star, HTTP/two point
zero, then two blank lines, SM,

330
00:18:46,334 --> 00:18:48,195
and two more blank lines.

331
00:18:48,475 --> 00:18:50,209
Exactly twenty-four octets.

332
00:18:50,489 --> 00:18:55,806
An HTTP one point one server sees method
PRI, version two point zero,

333
00:18:55,806 --> 00:19:01,355
and fails cleanly with a five oh one,
instead of parsing binary as text.

334
00:19:01,635 --> 00:19:04,578
PRI and SM spell PRISM, from twenty thirteen.

335
00:19:04,899 --> 00:19:10,790
Over TLS, the protocol is negotiated with
ALPN, RFC seventy-three oh one.

336
00:19:11,070 --> 00:19:15,360
The client lists h2 and http one
point one in the ClientHello,

337
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:17,851
the server picks in the ServerHello.

338
00:19:18,131 --> 00:19:19,756
Zero extra round trips.

339
00:19:20,099 --> 00:19:25,304
For cleartext, h2c, the Upgrade dance was
deprecated in ninety-one thirteen.

340
00:19:25,584 --> 00:19:30,778
Only prior knowledge remains, and no browser
ever shipped cleartext HTTP two.

341
00:19:31,699 --> 00:19:32,803
Then the frame header.

342
00:19:33,083 --> 00:19:34,013
Nine bytes.

343
00:19:34,333 --> 00:19:35,731
Length, twenty-four bits.

344
00:19:36,011 --> 00:19:36,803
Type, eight.

345
00:19:37,083 --> 00:19:38,038
Flags, eight.

346
00:19:38,318 --> 00:19:39,481
One reserved bit.

347
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:41,829
And a thirty-one bit stream identifier.

348
00:19:42,109 --> 00:19:43,120
Then the payload.

349
00:19:43,466 --> 00:19:48,161
Client-initiated streams are odd,
server-initiated streams are even,

350
00:19:48,161 --> 00:19:50,163
so no coordination is needed.

351
00:19:50,443 --> 00:19:54,537
Stream zero is the connection itself:
SETTINGS, PING and GOAWAY.

352
00:19:54,866 --> 00:20:00,065
Example: a GET/request is sixty-seven bytes of
text, and fifteen bytes with HPACK.

353
00:20:00,999 --> 00:20:02,473
There are ten frame types.

354
00:20:03,499 --> 00:20:07,058
DATA carries body chunks, replacing
Content-Length and chunked.

355
00:20:07,399 --> 00:20:10,126
HEADERS opens a stream, with an HPACK block.

356
00:20:10,466 --> 00:20:14,731
PRIORITY is deprecated, replaced by
RFC ninety-two eighteen.

357
00:20:15,066 --> 00:20:20,780
RST_STREAM cancels one stream, instead of
closing TCP and killing everything.

358
00:20:21,099 --> 00:20:23,624
SETTINGS announces limits in both directions.

359
00:20:23,966 --> 00:20:25,639
PUSH_PROMISE is server push.

360
00:20:25,919 --> 00:20:26,990
It did not last.

361
00:20:27,333 --> 00:20:30,771
PING measures round trip time and
keeps intermediaries awake.

362
00:20:31,099 --> 00:20:34,532
GOAWAY says: this is the last
stream id I will process.

363
00:20:34,812 --> 00:20:35,930
A graceful drain.

364
00:20:36,266 --> 00:20:39,744
WINDOW_UPDATE gives flow control per
stream and per connection.

365
00:20:40,066 --> 00:20:43,654
And CONTINUATION carries a header
block bigger than one frame.

366
00:20:43,934 --> 00:20:47,282
It became a denial of service class
in twenty twenty-four.

367
00:20:47,599 --> 00:20:51,561
Defaults to remember: header table
size four thousand ninety-six,

368
00:20:51,561 --> 00:20:55,523
initial window size sixty-five
thousand five hundred thirty-five,

369
00:20:55,523 --> 00:20:59,058
max frame size sixteen thousand
three hundred eighty-four,

370
00:20:59,058 --> 00:21:04,421
and max concurrent streams unlimited, though no
smaller than one hundred is recommended.

371
00:21:05,366 --> 00:21:08,018
HPACK, RFC seventy-five forty-one.

372
00:21:08,298 --> 00:21:09,906
It uses three mechanisms.

373
00:21:10,233 --> 00:21:10,735
One.

374
00:21:11,015 --> 00:21:14,473
The static table, sixty-one
entries, identical everywhere.

375
00:21:14,753 --> 00:21:20,193
Index two is method GET, name and value, so a
GET costs one byte: zero x eighty-two.

376
00:21:20,473 --> 00:21:21,420
Not compressed.

377
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:22,405
Indexed.

378
00:21:22,733 --> 00:21:23,269
Two.

379
00:21:23,549 --> 00:21:25,979
The dynamic table, from index sixty-two.

380
00:21:26,259 --> 00:21:29,253
Anything sent before on this
connection gets an index.

381
00:21:29,533 --> 00:21:34,509
A three hundred byte cookie costs three hundred
bytes once, then one or two bytes forever.

382
00:21:34,833 --> 00:21:35,419
Three.

383
00:21:35,699 --> 00:21:41,189
Huffman coding, from Appendix B: a fixed
canonical code built from real header text.

384
00:21:41,469 --> 00:21:44,230
The letter e takes five bits, and X takes eight.

385
00:21:44,510 --> 00:21:46,935
It is used per string, only when it shrinks it.

386
00:21:49,266 --> 00:21:54,650
The same header set: one hundred seventy-four
bytes as HTTP one point one text.

387
00:21:54,999 --> 00:21:57,678
Seventy-nine bytes the first time with HPACK.

388
00:21:57,958 --> 00:21:59,495
Fifty-five percent saved.

389
00:21:59,833 --> 00:22:01,532
And six bytes the second time.

390
00:22:01,812 --> 00:22:03,355
Six one-byte indices.

391
00:22:03,635 --> 00:22:05,207
Ninety-seven percent saved.

392
00:22:05,533 --> 00:22:07,586
The dynamic table is per connection.

393
00:22:07,866 --> 00:22:12,751
So HTTP two wants one connection, and
domain sharding is now harmful.

394
00:22:13,699 --> 00:22:15,536
So why not just gzip the headers?

395
00:22:15,816 --> 00:22:17,752
Because of CRIME, in twenty twelve.

396
00:22:18,099 --> 00:22:21,956
The attacker cannot decrypt anything, but
can watch the compressed size.

397
00:22:22,236 --> 00:22:24,627
They inject a guess next to a secret cookie.

398
00:22:24,966 --> 00:22:27,576
When the guess matches, gzip finds a repeat,

399
00:22:27,899 --> 00:22:29,660
and the length drops by one byte.

400
00:22:29,999 --> 00:22:31,493
That means the guess was right.

401
00:22:31,773 --> 00:22:33,241
Repeat for each character.

402
00:22:33,566 --> 00:22:35,376
HPACK's defence is structural.

403
00:22:35,656 --> 00:22:38,174
The Huffman table is fixed, not adaptive.

404
00:22:38,454 --> 00:22:42,716
The dynamic table is an exact-match
index, not a substring matcher.

405
00:22:42,996 --> 00:22:46,993
And section seven point one lets you
mark fields as never indexed.

406
00:22:47,333 --> 00:22:48,055
A warning:

407
00:22:48,055 --> 00:22:51,447
gzipping a JSON response that contains a user's

408
00:22:51,447 --> 00:22:54,767
search term next to an API key rebuilds CRIME.

409
00:22:55,047 --> 00:22:56,690
That is the BREACH style of attack.

410
00:22:57,633 --> 00:22:58,905
And now multiplexing.

411
00:22:59,185 --> 00:23:01,839
The same six requests, over one connection.

412
00:23:02,166 --> 00:23:05,407
Style dot css in four point nine milliseconds.

413
00:23:05,733 --> 00:23:07,500
The logo in five point seven.

414
00:23:07,833 --> 00:23:09,590
The icon in six point nine.

415
00:23:09,933 --> 00:23:12,179
App dot js in six point nine.

416
00:23:12,499 --> 00:23:14,585
The hero image in seven point eight.

417
00:23:14,933 --> 00:23:18,533
And big dot txt in two thousand five milliseconds.

418
00:23:18,813 --> 00:23:22,949
Out of order, on purpose, because each frame
says which stream it belongs to.

419
00:23:23,266 --> 00:23:27,524
One honest lesson from the demo: a
protocol can only give you permission.

420
00:23:27,804 --> 00:23:31,717
The first version handled requests
inline in the read loop,

421
00:23:31,717 --> 00:23:37,553
and got exactly HTTP one point one's numbers, all
around two thousand five milliseconds.

422
00:23:37,833 --> 00:23:41,107
The fix was a thread per stream and
a lock on the connection.

423
00:23:41,387 --> 00:23:42,643
The transport was fine.

424
00:23:42,923 --> 00:23:44,785
We serialised the work ourselves.

425
00:23:45,065 --> 00:23:49,491
The same shape: a worker pool sharing
one database connection,

426
00:23:49,491 --> 00:23:54,558
a blocking call inside a coroutine, a
gRPC handler with a global mutex.

427
00:23:55,499 --> 00:23:56,352
Server push.

428
00:23:56,632 --> 00:23:58,073
Nobody deprecated it.

429
00:23:58,353 --> 00:23:59,709
Everybody removed it.

430
00:24:00,033 --> 00:24:04,156
It is still in RFC ninety-one
thirteen and ninety-one fourteen.

431
00:24:04,436 --> 00:24:07,957
Only PRIORITY and the h2c Upgrade were deprecated.

432
00:24:08,299 --> 00:24:12,977
Chrome one oh six, on the twenty-seventh of
September twenty twenty-two, removed it.

433
00:24:13,257 --> 00:24:19,234
It was used by one point two five percent of
HTTP two sites, later zero point seven.

434
00:24:19,514 --> 00:24:23,264
No clear net performance gain, and
in many cases regressions.

435
00:24:23,599 --> 00:24:26,543
The problem: the server does not
know what is in your cache.

436
00:24:26,823 --> 00:24:31,469
It pushes files you already have, competing
for bandwidth and congestion window.

437
00:24:31,799 --> 00:24:36,364
The replacement is one oh three Early
Hints, RFC eighty-two ninety-seven.

438
00:24:36,644 --> 00:24:39,865
The server says: you are going
to want style dot css.

439
00:24:40,145 --> 00:24:42,290
The client checks its cache, and decides.

440
00:24:42,570 --> 00:24:45,739
The server keeps the knowledge, the
client keeps the decision.

441
00:24:46,019 --> 00:24:47,377
Inform, do not act.

442
00:24:48,299 --> 00:24:52,625
What HTTP two could not fix: TCP
head-of-line blocking.

443
00:24:52,966 --> 00:24:56,737
RFC ninety-one fourteen, section one point one:

444
00:24:56,737 --> 00:25:00,429
a lost or reordered packet stalls all streams,

445
00:25:00,429 --> 00:25:05,082
because TCP's loss recovery cannot
see HTTP two's streams.

446
00:25:05,399 --> 00:25:06,094
In the demo,

447
00:25:06,094 --> 00:25:09,682
a three hundred millisecond stall at
byte three thousand makes

448
00:25:09,682 --> 00:25:13,675
everything behind the hole wait about
three hundred six milliseconds,

449
00:25:13,675 --> 00:25:16,106
even though the bytes had already arrived.

450
00:25:16,386 --> 00:25:18,688
The kernel cannot hand them over out of order.

451
00:25:18,968 --> 00:25:21,262
Which streams get caught changes every run.

452
00:25:21,599 --> 00:25:26,838
Google's SPDY data: an eleven point eight
one percent speedup at zero loss,

453
00:25:26,838 --> 00:25:30,750
and forty-seven point seven
percent at two percent loss.

454
00:25:31,030 --> 00:25:34,664
Multiplexing helps most exactly
where TCP hurts most.

455
00:25:35,599 --> 00:25:36,354
Now QUIC.

456
00:25:36,634 --> 00:25:37,713
Why UDP?

457
00:25:37,993 --> 00:25:40,866
Because the internet stopped
accepting new protocols.

458
00:25:41,199 --> 00:25:48,186
IP protocol numbers: TCP is six, UDP is
seventeen, SCTP is one thirty-two.

459
00:25:48,466 --> 00:25:51,145
Why not just pick a new number,
like two fifty-three?

460
00:25:51,466 --> 00:25:54,471
Because every NAT, firewall, carrier grade NAT,

461
00:25:54,471 --> 00:25:58,690
corporate proxy and hotel router drops
what it does not recognise.

462
00:25:58,970 --> 00:26:04,515
SCTP, standardised in two thousand, cannot be
used across the public internet.

463
00:26:04,795 --> 00:26:07,233
UDP is a hole that already exists.

464
00:26:07,566 --> 00:26:09,664
The second reason is ossification.

465
00:26:09,944 --> 00:26:13,373
Anything a middlebox can read, it
will eventually depend on.

466
00:26:13,653 --> 00:26:15,928
That is RFC eighty-nine ninety-nine.

467
00:26:16,208 --> 00:26:19,119
It is how TCP options became unusable.

468
00:26:19,399 --> 00:26:23,807
QUIC encrypts packet numbers and most of
its header, not only for privacy,

469
00:26:23,807 --> 00:26:25,177
but to stay changeable.

470
00:26:25,499 --> 00:26:31,380
And userspace: a TCP change needs a kernel
upgrade on a billion machines and a decade.

471
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:33,295
A QUIC change ships with your app.

472
00:26:34,233 --> 00:26:35,113
Round trips.

473
00:26:35,393 --> 00:26:36,900
One handshake, not two.

474
00:26:37,233 --> 00:26:44,315
A cold connection with TCP, TLS one point three
and HTTP two takes three round trips.

475
00:26:44,595 --> 00:26:46,714
TLS one point two would be four.

476
00:26:46,994 --> 00:26:51,601
Measured at one hundred fifty milliseconds:
four hundred fifty-six point six.

477
00:26:51,933 --> 00:26:56,783
QUIC with HTTP three needs two round trips:
the handshake, and the request.

478
00:26:57,063 --> 00:26:59,529
Three hundred nineteen point eight milliseconds.

479
00:26:59,866 --> 00:27:05,340
And QUIC zero RTT, on a resumed connection,
sends the request in the first flight.

480
00:27:05,620 --> 00:27:06,612
One round trip.

481
00:27:06,892 --> 00:27:08,735
One hundred sixty-one point seven.

482
00:27:09,066 --> 00:27:11,968
RFC ninety-oh-oh-one, section two point one:

483
00:27:11,968 --> 00:27:15,530
there are no separate transport
and crypto handshakes.

484
00:27:15,810 --> 00:27:20,852
The TLS ClientHello rides in a QUIC CRYPTO
frame, and ALPN rides there too.

485
00:27:21,132 --> 00:27:22,776
TLS is mandatory.

486
00:27:23,056 --> 00:27:24,789
There is no unencrypted QUIC.

487
00:27:25,133 --> 00:27:28,205
Cold, QUIC saves one hundred
thirty-seven milliseconds.

488
00:27:28,485 --> 00:27:32,290
Resumed with zero RTT, it saves
two hundred ninety-five.

489
00:27:32,570 --> 00:27:35,748
Every layer boundary is a place
you may pay a round trip.

490
00:27:36,666 --> 00:27:39,269
Zero RTT has a price: replay.

491
00:27:39,599 --> 00:27:45,719
RFC ninety-oh-oh-one, section nine point two:
early data is exposed to replay attacks.

492
00:27:45,999 --> 00:27:49,432
Protections like a single-use
ticket cache are imperfect,

493
00:27:49,432 --> 00:27:51,781
because a server farm has many servers.

494
00:27:52,061 --> 00:27:53,951
The attacker replays to a different one.

495
00:27:54,299 --> 00:27:57,023
So the rule is: idempotent requests only.

496
00:27:57,303 --> 00:27:58,209
A GET is fine.

497
00:27:58,489 --> 00:28:00,598
A POST that charges a card is not.

498
00:28:00,933 --> 00:28:06,367
Cloudflare allows only GET and HEAD in zero
RTT, and sets an Early-Data header,

499
00:28:06,367 --> 00:28:09,874
so the origin can reply four
twenty-five Too Early.

500
00:28:10,199 --> 00:28:13,361
Zero RTT is a cache of a negotiation.

501
00:28:13,641 --> 00:28:18,948
And every cache of a negotiation trades a round
trip for the possibility of a replay.

502
00:28:19,866 --> 00:28:21,465
Head-of-line blocking, gone.

503
00:28:21,799 --> 00:28:24,633
QUIC STREAM frames carry a
stream id and an offset.

504
00:28:24,913 --> 00:28:28,126
They are ordered within a stream, and
unordered between streams.

505
00:28:28,466 --> 00:28:33,380
With eighteen datagrams deleted, five
percent, only big dot txt waited:

506
00:28:33,380 --> 00:28:35,249
seventy-eight milliseconds.

507
00:28:35,566 --> 00:28:37,249
The others took four to eleven.

508
00:28:37,566 --> 00:28:41,834
On TCP, the blast radius is whoever
had bytes behind the gap.

509
00:28:42,114 --> 00:28:44,239
On QUIC, it is exactly one stream.

510
00:28:45,166 --> 00:28:46,243
Connection IDs.

511
00:28:46,523 --> 00:28:48,928
A connection that survives changing networks.

512
00:28:49,266 --> 00:28:55,477
With TCP, the connection is the four-tuple:
source IP, source port, destination IP,

513
00:28:55,477 --> 00:28:56,749
destination port.

514
00:28:57,029 --> 00:29:02,210
Switch from Wi-Fi to LTE, the IP
changes, and every connection dies.

515
00:29:02,490 --> 00:29:05,969
Video buffers, downloads restart,
websockets reconnect.

516
00:29:06,249 --> 00:29:08,578
NAT rebinding after idle does the same.

517
00:29:08,899 --> 00:29:11,074
In QUIC, identity is not addressing.

518
00:29:11,354 --> 00:29:14,117
A connection ID identifies the connection.

519
00:29:14,397 --> 00:29:18,045
A new address, the same connection
ID, the path is validated,

520
00:29:18,045 --> 00:29:20,079
and you have connection migration.

521
00:29:20,399 --> 00:29:21,772
There is a privacy catch.

522
00:29:22,052 --> 00:29:25,435
A stable connection ID is a tracking identifier.

523
00:29:25,715 --> 00:29:30,530
So connection I Ds must not contain
information an observer can correlate,

524
00:29:30,530 --> 00:29:33,523
and endpoints issue a pool of them and rotate.

525
00:29:33,803 --> 00:29:36,917
Identity belongs at the layer
that owns the semantics.

526
00:29:37,866 --> 00:29:40,161
QPACK, RFC ninety-two oh four.

527
00:29:40,441 --> 00:29:42,681
HPACK had its own head-of-line blocking.

528
00:29:42,999 --> 00:29:47,935
HPACK's dynamic table is shared mutable
state that needs total ordering,

529
00:29:47,935 --> 00:29:49,374
which is free on TCP.

530
00:29:49,654 --> 00:29:50,762
QUIC removed that.

531
00:29:51,042 --> 00:29:54,130
So a header block on stream
twelve could reference an

532
00:29:54,130 --> 00:29:58,033
entry inserted on stream eight that has
not arrived yet, and stall.

533
00:29:58,366 --> 00:30:01,950
The fix: move table updates to
dedicated one-way streams.

534
00:30:02,230 --> 00:30:05,202
An encoder stream, type two, carries insertions.

535
00:30:05,482 --> 00:30:09,785
A decoder stream, type three, carries
acknowledgements: I have seen these.

536
00:30:10,065 --> 00:30:13,086
The encoder only references
entries known to be safe.

537
00:30:13,433 --> 00:30:17,401
Defaults: blocked streams zero, and
max table capacity zero.

538
00:30:17,681 --> 00:30:21,681
The static table: HPACK has sixty-
one entries from index one.

539
00:30:21,961 --> 00:30:24,804
QPACK has ninety-nine entries from index zero.

540
00:30:25,133 --> 00:30:29,770
The lesson: relax an ordering guarantee,
then go find what was relying on it.

541
00:30:30,699 --> 00:30:34,089
How does a browser find out a
server speaks HTTP three?

542
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:39,185
ALPN, RFC seventy-three oh one,
July twenty fourteen:

543
00:30:39,185 --> 00:30:42,549
the protocol is chosen in the TLS handshake.

544
00:30:42,829 --> 00:30:44,140
h2 or h3.

545
00:30:44,420 --> 00:30:45,993
But it needs a handshake first.

546
00:30:46,333 --> 00:30:50,870
Alt-Svc, RFC seventy-eight thirty-
eight, April twenty sixteen:

547
00:30:50,870 --> 00:30:57,423
a response header says h3 is available on port four
forty-three, and the browser remembers.

548
00:30:57,703 --> 00:31:02,234
The cost: one full TCP and TLS
connection per cold cache.

549
00:31:02,566 --> 00:31:09,374
And the HTTPS DNS record, RFC ninety-four
sixty, November twenty twenty-three.

550
00:31:09,654 --> 00:31:13,187
DNS record type sixty-five carries the ALPN list.

551
00:31:13,467 --> 00:31:16,236
It costs nothing, because the
lookup happens anyway.

552
00:31:16,566 --> 00:31:21,913
Three to five percent of networks block all
UDP, per RFC ninety-three oh eight.

553
00:31:22,193 --> 00:31:26,238
Of Google's clients, ninety-five point
three percent could use QUIC,

554
00:31:26,238 --> 00:31:30,342
and four point four percent were blocked
or had packet size problems.

555
00:31:30,666 --> 00:31:35,750
And fallback to TCP is a downgrade attack
by construction: drop the UDP,

556
00:31:35,750 --> 00:31:37,585
and wait for the fallback.

557
00:31:38,533 --> 00:31:41,718
Adoption, in twenty twenty-four
to twenty twenty-six.

558
00:31:42,066 --> 00:31:46,306
By request, HTTP one point one is
about fifteen percent,

559
00:31:46,306 --> 00:31:49,183
and HTTP two and up about eighty-five.

560
00:31:49,533 --> 00:31:55,667
By homepage: h1 is twenty-one to twenty-two
percent, h2 seventy to seventy-one,

561
00:31:55,667 --> 00:31:57,298
and h3 seven to nine.

562
00:31:57,578 --> 00:32:03,063
Alt-Svc advertising h3 is twenty-six
to twenty-eight percent,

563
00:32:03,063 --> 00:32:06,391
and the HTTPS DNS record nine to ten.

564
00:32:06,733 --> 00:32:11,893
Fifteen percent of requests are still HTTP
one point one, eleven years later.

565
00:32:12,173 --> 00:32:14,564
There is no cutover, only a long tail.

566
00:32:15,499 --> 00:32:17,055
Four ideas from this session.

567
00:32:17,399 --> 00:32:17,902
One.

568
00:32:18,182 --> 00:32:20,032
Multiplexing needs identity.

569
00:32:20,312 --> 00:32:23,514
With no request id, the order is the identifier.

570
00:32:23,794 --> 00:32:31,038
Thirty-one bits fixed it: like TCP ports, TCAP
transaction ids, FastCGI request ids,

571
00:32:31,038 --> 00:32:32,763
and QUIC stream ids.

572
00:32:33,099 --> 00:32:33,636
Two.

573
00:32:33,916 --> 00:32:35,737
Every fix is the next problem.

574
00:32:36,017 --> 00:32:39,732
Persistence led to pipelining, which
led to head-of-line blocking.

575
00:32:40,012 --> 00:32:45,149
HTTP two fixed it above TCP and
exposed it inside TCP.

576
00:32:45,429 --> 00:32:48,629
QUIC fixed that, and broke
HPACK, which led to QPACK.

577
00:32:48,966 --> 00:32:49,552
Three.

578
00:32:49,832 --> 00:32:51,564
Semantics and encoding differ.

579
00:32:51,844 --> 00:32:54,046
RFC ninety-one ten is meaning.

580
00:32:54,326 --> 00:32:58,185
Ninety-one twelve, thirteen and fourteen
are three ways to write it.

581
00:32:58,465 --> 00:33:01,050
GET is unchanged since nineteen ninety-six.

582
00:33:01,399 --> 00:33:01,953
Four.

583
00:33:02,233 --> 00:33:04,401
Deployed reality beats specification.

584
00:33:04,681 --> 00:33:06,651
Three oh two led to three oh seven.

585
00:33:06,931 --> 00:33:08,617
Two connections became six.

586
00:33:08,897 --> 00:33:10,703
Postel's law lost to smuggling.

587
00:33:10,983 --> 00:33:12,531
Four eighteen is reserved.

588
00:33:12,811 --> 00:33:14,510
Push died without deprecation.

589
00:33:14,790 --> 00:33:18,473
And QUIC runs on UDP because
middleboxes have a veto.

590
00:33:19,399 --> 00:33:22,767
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session five.

591
00:33:52,366 --> 00:33:53,596
That is session five.

592
00:33:53,876 --> 00:33:56,660
Next up, session six: abusing CDNs.

